{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c407","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c40c-7a6a-7610-adaa-109852fda585"},"sequence":94,"recorded_at":1780531400000183,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"be2f90a4-329e-4dfe-906d-93614b40b7f2","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"be2f90a4-329e-4dfe-906d-93614b40b7f2","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"be2f90a4-329e-4dfe-906d-93614b40b7f2"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0c40c-e2e9-70f3-a157-8a5d0cce835a"},"task_id":"01a0c40c-e2e9-70f3-a157-8a5d0cce835a","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c40c-e2e9-70f3-a157-8a5d0cce835a","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-35urbp/TASK-260830-35urbp_rework-rev2.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-35urbp_producer.md. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-35urbp, the FIRST of three leaves of STORY-260830-2t4g7i. Revision 1 was reviewed by RUN-260921-0245e5 (claude-opus-5 max) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: two P1 classes, four P2, four P3. Read TASK-260830-35urbp_review-verdict-rev1.md in full before touching anything — it names exact files, line numbers and reproducing tests for every finding, and its measurements were taken on immutable copies of your exact tree. Do not re-litigate them; they were driven, not read.\n3|Trunk has NOT moved (base is still 799c338). Your working tree is the rev1 candidate; build revision 2 on it. Do not refresh, do not rebase.\n4|\n5|1. **P1-A — the candidate does not compile for Windows.** `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...` fails: `internal/tmuxserver/runtime_test.go` reassigns the `effectiveUID` seam that only `ownership_unix.go` (`//go:build !windows`) declares, and the file carries no build constraint. Trunk is clean under that gate; your package is the only failure. Constrain the seam-using tests, and prove it by running `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...` yourself and quoting the exit in the results. Note the trap that hid this: the local suite runs `GOOS=windows go build`, which compiles no test files, so construction stayed green. Assume nothing from a green construction about Windows test compilation.\n6|2. **P1-B — you forked a landed gate with a weaker copy, and the copy admits what the spec forbids.** This is rejection pattern (c), and the brief named it. `readiness.go` re-models the §4.2 credential-realm decision as loose fields (`LiveAttestation`, `CachedObservation`, `ManagerNameHint`, `BrokerStatus.Attested`) whose \"binding\" arm only checks three strings are non-empty. The repository already owns this decision: `internal/terminalbackend.Reconcile`/`Registry.AdmitProbe` admit the `credential_capable_execution_realm` Capability Evidence row with signature verification, liveness, generation binding (`CodeStaleGeneration`), `os_version`, `provider_build`, `sentinel_result`, `provider_auth_smoke_result`; `internal/axpane.checkRealm` already applies exactly that to the §4.2 background-caller rule; and `internal/termbind.ResolveEvidence` — the predecessor you were told to sit under — returns the `terminalbackend.Admitted` you should have consumed. Worse, `internal/axpane`'s `Realm` type documents in its own comments the design decision your copy reverses: no member for a cached sentinel, a managername observation or an attested-server boolean, because none authorizes. The reviewer drove three holes through your production entry `Acquire`: a request for `generation-7` attaches to a server attested for `generation-6` with yesterday's build and macOS (foreground), the same on the broker path, and a `CachedObservation` copied field-by-field into `LiveAttestation` authorizes attach. Fix it as a design change, not a patch: take the landed admission as input and decide \"attested\" from it composed with the generation equality axpane already enforces. The broker \"authenticated\" fact is new and may stay, but §4.4 requires it same-user and generation-bound, so it must be a typed, bound fact and not a bare boolean. Then re-derive evidence rows 24-39 from the composed gate, and state as an explicit BOUND whatever this leaf still cannot prove without a live broker. Rows 32-33 are vacuous today because `Request.Hint` and `Request.Cached` have zero production reads — do not replace them with another pair of fields that exist only to be proven unused.\n7|3. **P2-A — nine narrowing mutants survive your committed suite on gates your matrix counts closed.** Each was run twice against the suite as shipped and again with a probe that kills it, so every one is a real narrowing. Close them or state each as a bound, and add the harness row that proves the closure: `Acquire` swallows every commit-phase custody refusal (acquire.go:137, rows 4-9 are pinned at `EnsureRuntimeDir` only, no Acquire test stages a runtime-dir refusal); the verify side follows a symlink leaf (runtime_unix.go:107 — your `N-containment-symlink` mutates the commit side only); the override gate admits `TMUX_TMPDIR` (socket.go:86 — rows 19-22 pin four literals of one gate and miss the fifth member, the one tmux actually uses to relocate its default socket directory); the collision gate admits the inherited, TMUX-env, TMPDIR and conventional members one at a time (socket.go:108 — row 23 is pinned on `DefaultPath` only); the argv gate admits the TMUX environment value (argv.go:17 — row 47 says \"4 subtests\" and pins four of five members, reached only by a direct `BuildArgv` call); and the post-`Mkdirat` `Fchmod` arm is unobserved because the suite runs under umask 022 where `mkdirat` alone already yields 0700, so row 1's \"enforced explicitly because mkdir honors umask\" measures nothing. Also delete or implement `ObserveAmbient`'s unreachable \"ambient vocabulary\" refusal (socket.go:52-54): it guards two package constants that are valid env names, and row 18's sentence describes no behaviour. What the reviewer found genuinely pinned — the no-fallback rule along the caller, credential and platform axes — is closed; keep it.\n8|4. **P2-B — the Windows arm claims tmux where §4.3 forbids it** (SPEC 1458: \"Native Windows MUST NOT claim tmux or tmux resurrection\"). `runtime_windows.go` creates an `ax-tmux` runtime directory with `os.MkdirAll` (building parents, which the unix arm's own comment forbids), with no mode gate and no ownership gate, and calls the difference a \"custody subset\" bound. Refuse `PlatformWindows` with a typed refusal on every host, delete the Windows commit/verify arms and the tripwire, drop the `_ = filepath.Clean` import-keeping hack, and keep the package compiling and vetting under `GOOS=windows`.\n9|5. **P2-C — state the split with the lifecycle leaf as a BOUND.** `Dependencies` has no production implementation anywhere in the tree and nothing execs a server, yet the comment says \"Production wires live tmux and broker probes\". Write down that this leaf ships the decision core and argv only, that the exec/probe adapters and the server socket's crash evidence are owned by TASK-260830-1c28dz, and delete the sentence that describes code which does not exist. A stated bound is acceptable; an implied one is a finding.\n10|6. **P2-D — dead inputs, dead arms, a doc comment that contradicts the code.** `Request.NeedsCredentials`, `Hint` and `Cached` have no production reads. `acquireForeground`'s `checkCreationAllowed` call can never refuse because the caller was already classified foreground. `VerifyRuntimeDir` has zero production callers while its doc says \"Acquisition calls this instead of Ensure\", and `Acquire` calls `EnsureRuntimeDir` on every path — including the background path, so a background caller performs the durable directory write. Either wire Verify into the authorizing path as the comment promises, or delete both the comment and the entry; do not leave the two disagreeing.\n11|7. **P3-A** — the results claim the worktree config \"carries no `validation.commands` section (count 0, verified by read)\". The 30 commands are at `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands`; you did not run the configured suite, construction did. Read the right key and report what you actually ran. **P3-B** — crash convergence is umask-conditional: a leaf left between `Mkdirat` and `Fchmod` under an owner-bit-stripping umask refuses `runtime mode` on every retry and never converges, because existing leaves are verified and never repaired. State the bound (umask ⊆ 0077) or narrow the repair to owner-only, narrower-than-0700 leaves owned by the caller. **P3-C** — a symlinked intermediate component inside the root path is followed; only the root's last component refuses. That matches secprim's own root open and is acceptable AS A STATED BOUND; TRACEABILITY says nothing today. **P3-D** — `BuildArgv`'s `secprim.CheckArgv` refusal is uncovered; cover it or bound it.\n12|\n13|PROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta on the Story branch. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Run your own package tests, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once, reading the commands from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` in the candidate config (30 of them). Update the results with a finding-by-finding table (finding -> change -> the test that fails without it -> evidence path), update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive (a real gzip archive, MANIFEST with no self-entry, one raw log per plant with its subprocess exit, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, zero `__pycache__`), re-complete the checklist, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-35urbp --role developer` so revision 2 is constructed, and exit promptly. Report the ratio you MEASURE. Hand off no later than 3 hours in. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The same claude-opus-5 max reviewer contract applies to revision 2.\n14|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n15|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n16|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n17|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n18|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n19|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n20|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n21|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","final_result":true}}}
